Why the source is copied rather than imported as a module
---------------------------------------------------------
The shamir package has no go.mod of its own, so it belongs to the
github.com/hashicorp/vault module. That module's repository LICENSE is
BUSL-1.1, while upstream scopes this directory to MPL-2.0 with both a
per-file SPDX header and its own shamir/LICENSE, copied here unchanged.
Importing the package would place a BUSL-1.1 module in go.mod for the sake
of two files that are MPL-2.0. Copying the MPL-2.0 files, with their headers
and the MPL text, keeps the licence position unambiguous and keeps the
dependency graph small. The files are redistributed unmodified and remain
under MPL-2.0; they are not relicensed under this project's MIT licence.

Keeping the copy current
------------------------
Because this is a copy, "go get -u" does not touch it. CI runs a drift check
that fetches the upstream files and diffs them against the copy, so an upstream
change fails the build instead of going unnoticed. To sync deliberately:

  curl -sL https://raw.githubusercontent.com/hashicorp/vault/main/shamir/shamir.go \
    -o internal/shamir/shamir.go
  curl -sL https://raw.githubusercontent.com/hashicorp/vault/main/shamir/shamir_test.go \
    -o internal/shamir/shamir_test.go
  curl -sL https://raw.githubusercontent.com/hashicorp/vault/main/shamir/LICENSE \
    -o internal/shamir/LICENSE

Then review the diff, run the tests, and update the commit recorded above.
